  },
  "content": {
    "id": "p14-mastery-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "",
    "prompt": "In each of the following questions, determine whether to use the HCF or LCM to solve the question.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p14-q17",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "A dentist advises a patient to have a scale every 4 months and a polish every 6 months.\nIf they have a scale and polish today, how long will they wait before they can have both during the same appointment?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "LCM; 12 months.",
          "worked": "Use the LCM to find when both treatments are next due together.\n\nMultiples of $4$: $4,8,12,\\ldots$\n\nMultiples of $6$: $6,12,18,\\ldots$\n\nThe smallest shared multiple is $12$, so the wait is 12 months.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 26
      },
      {
        "id": "p14-q18",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Sophie checks her bike for repairs every 9 days and Susan checks hers every 12 days.\nIf they both check their bikes today, after how many days do they next both check their bikes again?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "LCM; 36 days.",
          "worked": "Use the LCM to find the next shared checking day.\n\nMultiples of $9$: $9,18,27,36,\\ldots$\n\nMultiples of $12$: $12,24,36,\\ldots$\n\nThe smallest shared multiple is $36$, so they next both check their bikes after 36 days.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 26
      },
      {
        "id": "p14-q19",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "A carpenter got given some free wooden planks. Some are 12 m long and some are 18 m long.\nShe wants to cut them up so that she has equal size planks to make using them easier.\nWhat is the greatest equal length she can cut them into to avoid wasting any wood?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "HCF; 6 m planks.",
          "worked": "Use the HCF to find the greatest length that divides both plank lengths exactly.\n\nFactors of $12$: $1,2,3,4,6,12$.\n\nFactors of $18$: $1,2,3,6,9,18$.\n\nThe largest shared factor is $6$, so she should cut the planks into 6 m lengths.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 26
      },
      {
        "id": "p14-q20",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "A bus company has 21-seater and 35-seater buses.\nWhat is the smallest group which will fit exactly onto a whole number of buses of either type?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "LCM; 105 people.",
          "worked": "Use the LCM to find the smallest group that fills buses of either type exactly.\n\nMultiples of $21$: $21,42,63,84,105,\\ldots$\n\nMultiples of $35$: $35,70,105,\\ldots$\n\nThe smallest shared multiple is $105$. A group of 105 people fills five 21-seater buses or three 35-seater buses.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      },
      {
        "id": "p14-q21",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "Year 7 has 48 students. Year 8 has 90 students.\nEach year group must be separately divided into football teams so that all teams are the same size, and there are no people left over. What’s the largest team size?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "HCF; 6 students per team.",
          "worked": "Use the HCF to find the largest team size that divides both year groups exactly.\n\nFactors of $48$: $1,2,3,4,6,8,12,16,24,48$.\n\nFactors of $90$: $1,2,3,5,6,9,10,15,18,30,45,90$.\n\nThe largest shared factor is $6$, so each team can have 6 students.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      },
      {
        "id": "p14-q22",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "Tom and Claire both like to do short bursts of exercise that involve small breaks.\nTom does a short burst of exercise every 2 minutes, and Claire does hers every 3 minutes.\nIf they start doing their exercise at 9:00 am, when do they both do their exercises again?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "LCM; after 6 minutes, at 9:06 am.",
          "worked": "Use the LCM to find when their exercise bursts next coincide.\n\nMultiples of $2$: $2,4,6,\\ldots$\n\nMultiples of $3$: $3,6,9,\\ldots$\n\nThe smallest shared multiple is $6$. Six minutes after 9:00 am is 9:06 am.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      }
    ]
  },
